Patch 44. In the series since 2026-10-17, after patch 41 (43 on sdk/android-apk-libs).

AllocFixed (src/core/memory_posix.cpp) committed a range by trying mmap(MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE)
first; over a range a heap already reserved, which is almost every commit, that fails with
EEXIST, and then IsRangeFullyMapped read and parsed the whole of /proc/self/maps before the
mprotect. With a loaded game's mappings that is about 30 us per commit: 31.8 us on Linux x86-64
with 2000 other mappings in the process (a bench of 4096 one-page commits). On kernels before
4.17 the path was not taken at all until patch 38, so there it newly cost every commit; on the
Galaxy Tab S5e (4.9) the game's loads showed 66 us per guest allocation. On Linux (and Android) a
commit with an address now calls mprotect first, one system call; mprotect fails with ENOMEM
where part of the range is not mapped, and only then does the call go on to map it as before.

Desktop Linux changes: the same result for every commit, from mprotect alone. One difference in
a failure: for a range only partly mapped, which the heaps never commit, Linux's mprotect may
change the mapped part before it fails, where the old path changed nothing; the call fails
either way. macOS already took mprotect directly; Windows is not involved.

Test: the SDK's suite (1768 of 1768 on Linux; the heap cases commit over reserved, decommitted
and released pages). The bench: 31.8 us to 1.0 us per commit on Linux x86-64. TABLET

